500 c_emp=0.177; log10x=4.50 K_emp=4000 c_emp=0.210; log10x=5.00 K_emp>8000 c_emp_LB>0.191. **σ_eff/σ_pred extension to worst-case statistic**: 12 (anchor, K) cells: 0.720 / 0.728 / 0.606 / 0.816 (10⁴ at K=100/1000/4000/8000); 0.685 / 0.780 / 0.878 / 0.818 (10⁴·⁵); 0.836 / 0.906 / 0.840 / 0.931 (10⁵). Mean 0.796 ± 0.092, consistent with Thread 7 S241/S244 0.755 ± 0.06. The half-Gaussian-tail prediction K_pred OVERSHOOTS empirical K_emp by factor 1.7-3.4× at the two reachable points; ratio K_emp/K_pred ≈ 0.49-0.60 matches the GUE-variance correction (σ_eff/σ_pred)² ≈ 0.61 — the half-Gaussian tail uses Gaussian variance but the actual variance is reduced by the GUE pair-correlation factor. **Hypothesis test (slot 2 falsifier)**: if Galway-shape `c_emp = const`, K_emp at x=10⁵·⁵ ≈ 0.21·562·160 ≈ 18900; if Thread-7-shape `c_emp ~ √x/log²x`, K_emp ≈ 10⁵·⁵·log N / (π² · log² x) · const ≈ 700. **The two predictions differ by 28×** — slot 2's measurement at x=10⁵·⁵ (requiring K up to ~25000 zeros) decisively distinguishes them. **What this rules out**: nothing structural — slot 1 is a baseline empirical measurement. The c_emp values (0.18-0.21) are 5-50× tighter than typical Galway-style published constants but the empirical-vs-published gap is uninformative without literature audit (slot 3 target). **What this does NOT close**: (i) Galway-shape vs Thread-7-shape resolution (slot 2 target); (ii) HKM/primecount benchmark comparison — at x=10⁴ K_emp=1500 with R_at_rho dps=18 evaluator gives 0.65 sec/π(x), HKM at same x gives <1ms; the explicit-formula evaluator is a theoretical object not currently competitive with primecount; (iii) literature audit of Galway 2004 / Buthe 2018 published constants for proper c_published reference (slot 3 target); (iv) tighter c_emp via larger N or different K-milestone density (slot 2/3 follow-on). **Six falsifiers** (F1: N-dependence not captured at N=100; F2: sample-window dependence at 10% vs 100%; F3: slot-2 reveals neither Galway-shape nor Thread-7-shape; F4: M=4/dps=18 truncation effect vs M=8/dps=25; F5: K_galway(c=1) reference normalisation choice; F6: K_emp non-monotonicity in K-milestone choice). **Cites** E1.5 (per-query bit-content; the explicit-formula partial sum approximates the ~50% random portion), Thread 5 / S224 (zeros-shared-database amortisation framing), Thread 7 / S195+S240+S241+S244 (σ_pred formula, GUE pair-correlation factor 0.755 ± 0.06, named-exponent corollary), E6.1 (Galway 2004 K = O(x^{1/2+ε}) — slot 1 measures the empirical worst-case constant for this bound at finite x). **Cross-domain ingredient**: no new technique; reuses Thread 7's σ_pred formula and R_at_rho evaluator. CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md unchanged. **Closure mode**: NOT a closure — slot 1 is positive-direction baseline measurement. **Next-action (slot 2)**: extend zeros database from K=8000 to K=25000-30000 via incremental mpmath.zetazero (~3h background compute); re-run slot1_worst_case_K.py at --anchors 5.0,5.5 --K_max 25000; update c_emp scaling table from 2 to 4 anchors; the K_emp(x=10^5.5) measurement decides Galway-shape vs Thread-7-shape. See `experiments/analytic/galway_constant/{slot1_worst_case_K.py, slot1_traces.csv (1800 rows), slot1_summary.csv (15 rows), slot1_run.log, slot1_worst_case_K_results.md}`, `archive/sessions/session434_commit_p5_galway_baseline.md`. | 434 |
